<title>UK: UK's Clarke says fiscal stimulus could be damaging.</title>
<headline>UK's Clarke says fiscal stimulus could be damaging.</headline>
<dateline>LONDON 1996-11-26</dateline>
<text>
<p>Chancellor of the Exchequer Kenneth Clarke said a fiscal stimulus to the British economy at this stage would be just as damaging as letting go of monetary policy.</p>
<p>&quot;Consumer spending is strong and inflation remains in check,&quot; Clarke told Parliament in his budget speech. &quot;But a fiscal stimulus to the economy at this stage could be just as damaging as letting go of monetary policy.&quot;</p>
<p>He also said his budget will cut taxes paid by the ordinary family and would include measures to increase the tax yield from existing measures.</p>
<p>-- London Newsroom +44 171 542 7717.</p>
</text>
